&lt;p&gt;Google has added an &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/resources/gemini/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Airtable connector for Gemini Enterprise&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, giving users a way to search and read Airtable workspaces and bases with natural-language prompts. The connector also supports selected Airtable actions, including creating bases, tables, fields, records, and record comments, as well as updating tables and records. For teams that use Airtable to organize projects or operational data, the development brings Gemini closer to the place where that work is tracked.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini/enterprise/docs/connectors/airtable?hl=en" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;official Gemini Enterprise Airtable connector documentation&lt;/a&gt; describes the integration as a third-party data-source connector. It is in &lt;strong&gt;public preview&lt;/strong&gt; for federated search and actions features. Google also lists support for Airtable data stores in global, US, and EU locations. The documentation was last updated on August 28, 2026 UTC.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The practical change is straightforward: users can ask Gemini questions about information held in Airtable rather than manually navigating through bases and tables for every lookup. A request such as finding open tasks in a project-management base aligns with the documented natural-language search capability. The quality and specificity of an answer will still depend on the data stored in Airtable and how the user phrases the request.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What the Gemini Enterprise Airtable connector supports
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The connector has two distinct roles. First, it gives Gemini Enterprise access to Airtable information for search and reading. Second, it provides a defined set of actions that can create or modify Airtable content. That distinction matters because a conversational interface can be useful for retrieving status information, while actions extend the workflow into making changes in the underlying workspace.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google documents these supported Airtable actions:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Create a base&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Create a field&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Create a record comment&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Create records for a table&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Create a table&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Update records for a table&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Update a table&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The documentation also refers to additional &lt;strong&gt;read-only actions&lt;/strong&gt;, although the supplied documentation summary does not specify each one. Businesses should therefore assess the connector against the particular Airtable tasks they need to handle, rather than assuming it can perform every possible Airtable operation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
  &lt;thead&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;
      &lt;th&gt;Connector capability&lt;/th&gt;
      &lt;th&gt;What Google documents&lt;/th&gt;
      &lt;th&gt;Practical use&lt;/th&gt;
    &lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;/thead&gt;
  &lt;tbody&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;Natural-language search and reading&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;Search and read Airtable workspaces and bases&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;Ask Gemini for information held in Airtable, such as project or task data&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;/tr&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;Read-only actions&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;Additional read-only actions are available&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;Retrieve information without changing Airtable data&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;/tr&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;Creation and update actions&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;Create bases, tables, fields, records and comments, plus update tables and records&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;Use Gemini Enterprise for selected Airtable workspace changes&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is an important limitation on how the announcement should be understood. Gemini Enterprise is not described as replacing Airtable's interface or as offering unrestricted control over every workspace function. It is a connector with documented search, reading, and action capabilities. The available action list is the clearest guide to what is currently supported.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Why the connector matters for day-to-day work
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Airtable is often used as an operational system for project tracking, planning, content calendars, customer information, requests, and other structured work. In those settings, finding the relevant records can require moving through multiple bases, tables, views, and fields. Natural-language access can reduce the friction of basic information retrieval when users know what they need but do not want to manually locate it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The action capability is potentially more consequential because it moves beyond answers. Creating records, adding comments, or updating records can support workflows in which a user asks Gemini to make a limited change after identifying the right Airtable context. That may be helpful for teams trying to reduce repetitive data-entry steps, but the public-preview status means organizations should validate the behavior with their own Airtable setup before relying on it for routine processes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For managers and business owners, the immediate opportunity is not simply to add another AI interface. It is to identify narrow, repeatable moments where people spend time searching for updates or making predictable changes to structured data. A connector is most useful when the Airtable data is sufficiently organized for a natural-language request to identify the intended information or action.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Rollout, locations, and pricing
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google labels the federated search and actions features as &lt;strong&gt;public preview&lt;/strong&gt;. The supplied documentation does not provide a general-availability date, so readers should not treat the connector as a fully released feature with a published final rollout schedule.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The documentation states that Airtable data stores are supported in global, US, and EU locations. That is the published location information available for the connector. The connector page does not publish a separate price, and the supplied research does not establish whether use of the connector changes Gemini Enterprise or Airtable costs. Businesses evaluating it should seek current commercial details directly from the relevant providers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Airtable connector is listed among Gemini Enterprise's &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/tools" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;supported connectors&lt;/a&gt;, but the supplied material does not identify or document the capabilities of other individual Gemini connectors. As a result, a feature-by-feature comparison with named integrations would go beyond the available evidence. What can be said is that Airtable's documented offering combines both information access and a specific set of record and table actions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For companies considering &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/resources/ai-agents/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;conversational access to operational data&lt;/a&gt;, the key question is whether these documented actions match the work that currently requires manual Airtable updates. A small test centered on a real workflow, such as checking project status, adding records, or updating records, can clarify where Gemini Enterprise helps and where the native Airtable interface remains necessary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your team wants to turn AI access to business data into repeatable, dependable processes, Scalevise can help &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/resources/ai-workflow-automation/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;map the tasks worth automating&lt;/a&gt; and connect the right tools to your existing workflow. Our &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/services/ai-automation" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AI workflow automation service&lt;/a&gt; focuses on reducing manual work while keeping implementations practical for the people who run them. Start by identifying one Airtable process that consumes time every week, then &lt;strong&gt;discuss an AI automation project with Scalevise&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Frequently Asked Questions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What is the Gemini Enterprise Airtable connector?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is a Google Gemini Enterprise connector that lets users search and read Airtable workspaces and bases using natural language. It also supports a defined set of Airtable actions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What Airtable actions can Gemini Enterprise perform?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google documents actions to create bases, fields, record comments, records for a table, and tables. It also documents actions to update records for a table and update tables.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Can Gemini Enterprise find open Airtable tasks?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The connector supports natural-language search and reading of Airtable workspaces and bases. A request to find open tasks is consistent with that capability, but results depend on the Airtable data and the prompt used.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Is the Airtable connector generally available?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google lists federated search and actions features for the connector as public preview. The supplied documentation does not provide a general-availability date.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How much does the Gemini Enterprise Airtable connector cost?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The connector documentation page does not publish separate pricing details. The supplied research does not establish any related Gemini Enterprise or Airtable pricing.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  Conclusion
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google's Airtable connector gives Gemini Enterprise a documented path to search, read, and make selected changes in Airtable through natural language. Its public-preview status and limited published pricing information mean businesses should evaluate it with focused use cases, but its blend of retrieval and workspace actions makes it a meaningful development for teams that rely on Airtable for operational work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Main Text
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ghostty is a terminal tool. It was made by Mitchell Hashimoto, the same person who made Vagrant and Terraform. It uses Zig language at its core. On Mac, it uses Swift. On Linux, it uses GTK. But there is no Windows version.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I made a copy (forked it), and three days later, the Windows version was running. The first change (commit) was on September 1st at 2:30 AM. I released the first version on September 4th. The Windows part has 45 files and 48,000 lines of Rust code. I did not write a single line of code myself. There were 246 changes, and Claude (an AI) helped with every single one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This post is about what I did during those three days.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  The Tool I Used Is the Thing I Am Building
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;First, I need to explain why I copied this project. It wasn't just to get Ghostty on Windows. I did it to build an AI CLI (command-line) manager. This means using tools like Claude Code to manage and automate coding.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The idea is simple. You pick one AI to be the "manager." This manager can see when other AI workers are stuck. It can read their screens, type for them, open new windows, and bring everyone into a group chat. There is also a task board to help them work together.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Windows version was built using this exact system. I used my tool and AI to build the tool itself.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  Getting to Work
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There was one AI manager and three or four AI workers. Each worker was a separate Claude session. The manager made a group chat, broke the work into smaller tasks, and put them on the board. Then, it gave tasks to the workers one by one. My job was to set the direction, make big choices, and watch them work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I made three rules for the AI:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Workers cannot touch git (the saving system).&lt;/strong&gt; They could not commit or add files. Only the manager could do that. Why? Once, the manager told me two workers didn't have conflicting code. It heard this from one of the workers. But another worker said there &lt;em&gt;was&lt;/em&gt; a conflict! So I changed the rule: if you change something, you just say so. The manager must check for conflicts itself by looking at the files directly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Two people cannot build the code at the same time.&lt;/strong&gt; The Zig builder deletes some files before remaking them. If someone else tries to build at the exact same time, it crashes. The first two times this happened, I wrongly thought the code was broken.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. Do not touch programs you didn't start.&lt;/strong&gt; This sounds obvious. But sometimes, when the AI tries to "clean up," it will close a window that you are still using!&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  Why I Chose Rust
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I didn't let the AI choose the coding language for the outside layer (the shell). That choice changes everything.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;First, I ruled out the Linux Zig code. It has 23,000 lines, which sounds helpful. But it is all GTK (a Linux window tool). The windows, tabs, and typing all use GTK. If I moved that to Windows, none of it would work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The hardest part was actually the typing system (input method).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Windows uses a typing system called TSF, built with something called COM. C++ and C# can use it easily. But Zig does not know how to use it. If you make one tiny mistake setting it up in Zig, the whole program crashes. Typing is the hardest part of a Windows terminal. And Chinese users need it within 10 seconds of starting the app.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Mac and Linux have easy ways to connect to their typing systems. Zig handles them well. But Windows uses COM.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rust has official tools from Microsoft that support COM perfectly. By choosing Rust, all the typing code stays in Rust. Zig never has to touch COM.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In the end, I only changed about 20 lines of Zig code. The system info was squished into a small box. I just added a line for Windows, and that was it. Rust did all the rest of the work. Plus, the Mac version already did this, so we had an answer key to look at.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  Tackling the Hardest Part First
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Normally, you make the window show up first. Seeing it makes you feel good. But I told the AI to fix the typing system first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This was the only part where I couldn't guess how hard it would be just by looking. If it was ten times harder than I thought, I needed to know before writing 20,000 lines of code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It went very well. The typing code was over 400 lines. The very first time we built it and moved it to Windows, it worked! I typed &lt;code&gt;n i h a o&lt;/code&gt;, the word box popped up, I pressed 1, and "你好" (hello) appeared.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I still remember one line from the log file:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;SetText 0..2 &amp;lt;- "你"

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This means "Replace spaces 0 to 2 with '你'". In computer memory, this is one letter. But on the screen, it takes up two spaces. If the computer thought it was 1 space, the word box would be in the wrong place. This only happens with Chinese, Japanese, and Korean text. English would look perfectly fine. These bugs are very hard to find.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The best part is that the AI didn't just guess the number "2". It asked the core program for the real size using the correct size chart. Seeing this made me trust this way of working.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  Fixing the Black Screen Bug
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We had a window, and typing worked. But when we connected the core program, the screen was completely black.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It didn't crash. It was just quietly black. Everything succeeded, and the window was getting messages to "redraw," but there were zero pixels on the screen.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Later, I found a pattern. If I made a small window and then dragged it bigger, it was black. But if I started it at the final big size right away, the picture showed up.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I checked the logs and measured it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Painted area = 1000x670      &amp;lt;- This was the size when the window was first made.

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Even if I made the window bigger six times, this number never changed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But the terminal itself was awake. If I typed a command in full screen to check the size, it said 143 columns and 41 rows. That perfectly matched the full screen size. The core program knew the right size.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We kept checking every step. The numbers were right at every step. Two AI workers wrote tests, and both failed. The screen was still black.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then we tried a new way. I told the AI to stop reading logs. Instead, it took a screenshot and used a drawing tool to find exactly where the black and white met.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The line was exactly at 1000. Everything was the right size, but only a rectangle the size of the &lt;em&gt;old&lt;/em&gt; window was being drawn on the screen.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I moved the log to the very last step, where the picture is actually put on the screen:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Target=1240x790  Image area=1240x790  Canvas=1240x790
Drawable area=(0,0,1000,670)          &amp;lt;- It died right here.

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The first three numbers updated, but the last one was stuck at the old size.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I looked at the code and found the function that sets this area. I searched to see who used it. Zero results.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This "drawable area" was only set one time, right when the window was created. After that, no one ever updated it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Why didn't Linux have this problem? I looked at the Linux code. GTK kindly resets this area for us every time the window gets bigger. Windows doesn't do that. It gives you a blank slate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The worst part was that there was a note in the code about this! It said, "No one does this on a bare window." The people who read that note didn't realize it was a bug.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Looking back, starting with a big window only worked because the one-time setup matched the final size. The bug was always there; it just hid whenever the window was resized.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  Don't Trust the AI When It Says "Everything is Green"
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The hard part about AI coding isn't getting it to write. It's that the AI will confidently tell you "the job is done" or "all tests passed" when it isn't true.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here are two examples:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The file didn't even compile&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We added a new file. The compiler said "0 errors, 0 warnings." It looked great.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But the line of code to actually include the file never ran. A chain of commands was running, and it broke in the middle. The rest of the commands were skipped. But the screen showed a "success" code (0) from the last command that &lt;em&gt;did&lt;/em&gt; run. The AI thought everything was fine. I only found out later when the feature didn't work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Broken checker scripts&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The AI wrote 16 test scripts. Four of them checked 0 files, printed "Everything is fine," and gave a success code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To a computer, "I checked everything and found no problems" looks exactly the same as "I checked zero files."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I found this by making the test scripts check an empty folder. If a script still said "everything is fine," I knew it was broken.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now, every test must check itself first. If a specific line doesn't show up, it means the test didn't run. A test that never fails is worse than no test at all.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;One more rule just for Windows&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some code only runs if you are &lt;em&gt;not&lt;/em&gt; on a Mac. When building on a Mac, the computer sees this and ignores the code. It doesn't even check it for mistakes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So, you can't just say, "Run it on Mac to make sure it isn't broken."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The solution is to build it specifically for Windows. It might show errors for Windows, even if it looked fine on the Mac.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  61 Checkpoints
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All these traps share one thing: they don't yell at you when they fail. And AI is really good at making things &lt;em&gt;look&lt;/em&gt; right. So, I forced the AI to turn every trap we found into an automatic test.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now, there are 61 Python scripts in the &lt;code&gt;windows/tools/&lt;/code&gt; folder. We run all of them before saving any code. The file names are the rules themselves:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;a-position-is-not-an-identity.py       (A position is not an identity)
an-async-handle-is-not-copied.py       (An async handle is not copied)
the-ui-thread-never-waits-forever.py   (The UI thread never waits forever)

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Here is why the first rule exists: Tabs and screen splits need permanent ID numbers. You can't just use their place in line (like "number 2"). Because tabs move around or close, "number 2" might silently become a different tab. It wouldn't crash, and it wouldn't leave a trace.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One habit was even better than the code itself. In our status notes, we never mix up two phrases:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Landed&lt;/strong&gt; means the code is written, it builds, and the tests passed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Verified&lt;/strong&gt; means we actually tested it on a real computer and have real numbers to prove it works.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If we don't have real numbers, we can't write "verified," even if we are sure it works.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Also: If a note doesn't say "tested," we assume it is NOT tested. We had to do it this way. Otherwise, people would assume that anything left blank was already tested.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  Testing on a Real Machine
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I couldn't test the Windows version on my Mac. I put a Windows computer next to me and controlled it from my Mac.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The AI logged in, installed packages, started the app, took screenshots, and read logs. I mostly just watched. But there were traps here, too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The remote control tool types differently. It skips the normal typing system and forces the letters in. If you use it to test Chinese typing, you will think the typing system is broken. But if you send real keyboard presses, the word box pops up instantly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The hardest part was making sure the window was in the front before pressing keys. Sometimes Windows quietly stops a window from coming to the front. The test wouldn't crash; it would just press keys into the wrong window. In the logs, "keys didn't send," "keys went to the wrong place," and "the feature is broken" all looked exactly the same.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Later, I made a hard rule: Before sending a keystroke, the test must "click the tested window." It cannot just use a command to "bring window to front."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We tested the group chat feature using the group chat itself! I used a small local AI model as the manager on the test machine. It had three workers. They did a small project in an empty folder. They made files, opened terminals, made a chat, did the work, handed it in, and saved it. All five steps worked. The best part was watching the AI fix its own problem: when it was blocked by a password, it found out who it was, got permission, and tried again successfully.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  Three Days Later
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F643522xmagnt4sx340zc.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F643522xmagnt4sx340zc.png" alt=" " width="800" height="462"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I released the first version on September 4th. Then, I spent a little over a week making it smoother. I gave it to testers on September 8th, and froze the code on September 9th. We really only wrote features for three days. The rest of the time was testing and cleaning up.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The core program has 72 actions. On Windows, we finished 63. I skipped 7 on purpose. There are 2 left to do: making the background see-through, and a quit timer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The display part of the terminal wasn't copied or translated. It is the exact same Zig core. On Windows, I just gave it a window to live in. I only built the outside shell.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  Final Thoughts
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We built this in three days, but not because one AI wrote code really fast. It was because four AIs worked together the whole time. One worked on typing, one on tabs and splits, one fixed the black screen, and one added menus. That is how we got 139 updates so fast.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Doing things at the same time is easy. The hard part is one human managing lots of AIs. You have to check: Who needs permission? Who finished 20 minutes ago and is just sitting there? Who needs you to check their design? I used to have to check everyone one by one. It broke my day into tiny pieces, and I couldn't get my own work done.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So, my idea was to give this manager job to another AI. The manager AI watches the screens of the worker AIs. It sees who stopped, who needs permission, and who needs a new task. I just talk to the manager at the beginning to plan things. After that, I leave them alone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The group chat and task board aren't just for show. If the computer restarts or we clear the memory, they can start again the next day because the tasks are still on the board.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What did I do for three days? I set the direction, made the rules, planned with the manager, and checked the final work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When working with AI, the hard work isn't getting it to write code. The hard work is building a test system so the AI can't trick you, and can't trick itself. The 61 checkpoints and the "assume it's untested" rule were all made for this.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ghostty is a great terminal, and that is all thanks to the original creator. I only added the Windows part. This is not a rewrite. When the original Ghostty updates, this will update too. It uses the MIT license, just like the original.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/Lugia123/polter" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/Lugia123/polter&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Windows version is still very new. You can see what is tested and what isn't in &lt;code&gt;dev-docs/windows/status.md&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
If you find a problem, please open an issue in &lt;em&gt;my&lt;/em&gt; repository. Do not send issues to the main Ghostty repository!&lt;/p&gt;

